Since you mentioned Stirner, Browne, etc., in a recent post, I thought
I'd stick in my egoist analysis of the issue of self esteem. 

You quite reasonably reject acceptance of a person because they share
in the predicate Man. You consider self acceptance w/o self esteem an
improvement over self loathing, but prefer self acceptance through
self esteem.

While I consider self acceptance through self esteem superior to self
acceptance through sharing in some predicate (Man, rational being,
life, etc.,), I still consider it a trap. Instead of acting as the
subject doing the valuing, one treats oneself as an object to be
valued or disvalued. 

