Only (for instance) one funny letter agreed with you on this point but then
proceeded to disagree with you on specifics!

>It gets you nowhere to demand: "Oh yeah, *prove* to me that the apple isn't
>conscious!"

But it does get somewhere to say, "By consciousness, I mean ability to
exhibit certain behaviors."  It gets me to the point where, if something
acts conscious, I can tell whether it's conscious.  You imply there is
something more to the issue, but I don't remember you naming it.

> The burden of proof is on the affirmative to demonstrate, or at
>least to provide an argument or some good evidence for, the truth of a
>proposition.

I think there's a prior issue of deciding whether we have something to
talk about other than behavior.  I wouldn't argue that machines can have
the ineffable property you say exists.  I don't know what it is, or that
it exists in you or me.

