Dear Mr. Hawk,

As requested:

Pricing the Information Superhighway -- The 1994 InfoWorld Iway Poll

by Bob Metcalfe, Executive Correspondent, InfoWorld

How much will you pay to send electronic messages through the Information 
Superhighway?  How much to see whom you're talking to through videophones?  
These are among many pricing questions which arise when considering the 
Information Superhighway, not as a money-is-no-object national vision, like 
going to the moon, but as an economically justified tool of commerce.
    The following results of The 1994 InfoWorld Iway Poll offer early answers 
to these pricing questions and preliminary guidance for those developing policy 
for and planning investments in what Washington calls National Information 
Infrastructure.
    "Infrastructure" is a code word which means important stuff that will be 
paid for out of budgets other than our own, usually out of corporate overhead, 
or better yet, by some government.  Our poll takes the point of view that the 
Iway is an industry and its market, driving growth in the world economy, 
something worth paying for ourselves.

ECONOMICALLY CORRECT

Vice President Gore has succeeded in hyping everybody up about his Information 
Superhighway.  Count us among those who share Gore's enthusiasm for whatever 
the Iway turns out to be.
    Gore seeks a lot of advice about the Iway, for example Commerce Secretary 
Ron Brown's Advisory Council on National Information Infrastructure (NII).  
Those regularly consulted include professors, lobbyists, civil libertarians, 
bureaucrats, and the private sector represented by big /suppliers/ of the Iway.
    The most important people to the Iway's future, however, the business 
people who will eventually have to /buy/ the Iway for their companies' 
information systems, have not heretofore had much chance to get their two cents 
in.
    Prominent among potential Iway buyers are InfoWorld subscribers among whom 
our poll was conducted by the International Data Corporation between July 27 
and August 5.  We used a large enough random sample of our 225,000 subscribers 
to make poll results projectable, not to the general population, but to 
information systems managers, technically knowledgeable volume buyers of 
information technology.

GORE'S IWAY METAPHOR WORKS

Of course we couldn't just ask how much would you pay for the Iway.  Needless 
to say, there is considerable doubt about what the Iway is exactly.
    Denise Caruso, noted Iway analyst, is editorial director and publisher at 
the Technology & Media Group, Inc. in San Francisco, California.  Denise 
worries that pricing information is meaningless this early in the evolution of 
the Iway.  She does like the idea of going to technically sophisticated buyers 
who better understand the costs and related values of high-tech products.  We 
reassured her that we would not jump to conclusions on this first poll, that we 
would poll again as the Iway evolves, and that much of first poll's 28 
questions were spent on emerging notions of the Iway.
    The first result of The 1994 InfoWorld Iway Poll was a high response rate, 
70%, which eliminates our worst fear, that buyers of the Iway might be totally 
disinterested.
    When we asked about Gore's promotional efforts on NII, 68% said they are 
aware of them.  Gore should be pleased to learn that 30% of you are very 
supportive; another 52% are somewhat.
    This strong support is consistent with 85% of respondents finding the Iway 
to be important to overall U.S. industrial competitiveness.  92% say the U.S. 
is among the world leaders in developing the Iway.
    We asked about Gore's metaphor -- The Information Superhighway.  Well, 29% 
of InfoWorld subscribers find the metaphor to be "an exciting vision for what 
should clearly be a major U.S. priority."  Another 38% say the Iway metaphor 
"well characterizes the opportunities we are facing in networks."  A more 
critical 12% say that networks are "not all that much like automobile highways" 
and 17% say "Information Superhighway is a trite buzzword that should go away."
    So, two thirds of our subscribers have not been put off by all the cloying 
hype surrounding Gore's Iway vision.  It, that is the vision, works.
    Familiarity with terms related to Information Superhighway ran high on a 
scale from 1 (not familiar) to 10 (very familiar):

Bulletin Board Systems 8.3 (38% very)
Online Services 8.2 (32% very)
The Internet 7.4 (23% very)
Information Superhighway 6.1 (10% very)
Cyberspace 4.8 (8% very)
Information Infrastructure 4.6 (4% very)
Electronic Frontier 4.3 (6% very)
Information Supermarket  2.9 (1% very)

IS managers are familiar with the Iway, but surprisingly, they are most 
familiar with bulletin board systems and online services, much more so than 
with the term Iway or with the much ballyhooed Internet.  Let this be a BBS 
wake-up call for Iway market analysts.

500 CHANNELS NOT

When asked how the Iway will be accessed by "the average consumer," 79% of our 
IS respondents said personal computers, 11% TVs, and 5% telephones.  Should we 
conclude that the Iway's set-top boxes will eventually turn out less like TVs 
or telephones than today's personal computers?
    Following up on the relatively low number for TV access, we asked point 
blank, "Do you think your organization would be likely to take advantage of the 
availability of 500 channels of interactive video services?"  While 36% said 
yes, 48% said no.
    Caruso, who is unconvinced that 500 channels are a good model for the Iway, 
was heartened by these negatives results from business buyers.
    On the other hand, John McQuillan, noted Iway consultant  in Cambridge, 
Massachusetts, agrees the Iway will find its earliest uses filling the 
information needs of business buyers, but he cautions that communication and 
entertainment will eventually swamp information uses of the Iway.  We have to 
be careful that our general Iway model is not too focused on the early 
information uses, ignoring 500 channels of entertainment, for example.
    When offered ingeniously phrased alternatives for a general Iway model, 39% 
said the Iway should be "very much like the Internet but easier to use."  Right 
behind that, 33% said the Iway should be "a widely available, low cost, simple 
and largely passive network which is easy to build upon, sort of like the 
telephone network."  Only 14% chose "a diverse group of specific network 
services for specific fees, like a cable TV system."  And about the same 
number, 13%, said the Iway should be "similar to computer network services such 
as America Online, CompuServe, and Prodigy."
    There's that Internet back on top again.  Over 42% of respondents said that 
there is someone in their organization specifically responsible for using the 
Internet -- 9% are using an Internet consultant.

GOVERNMENT IN ITS PLACE

We asked Iway buyers to rate the appropriateness from 1 (not appropriate) to 10 
(very appropriate) of various rolls for Misters Gore, Brown, and "the U.S. 
federal government in developing this NII:"

Sponsor of research and development (6.9)
Guarantor of public access and fairness (6.7)
Protector of privacy (6.4)
Provider of funds for pilot projects (6.4)
Protector of security (6.2)
Guarantor of overall U.S. competitiveness and leadership (5.8)
Major investor in network infrastructure (5.4)
Leading edge user demonstrating what can be done (5.3)
Seed money investor in promising companies (5.2)
Creator of a dynamic competitive climate (5.1)
Network standards setter (4.9)
Regulator of rates (4.9)
Protector of decency (4.4)
Principal network planner, builder, operator (3.2)

WHO WILL BUILD IT?

One determinant of success in a market is whether buyers feel comfortable 
buying products from you in that market.  For example, Mattel would not likely 
do well in the parachute market.  So, we asked prospective Iway buyers to rate 
the importance from 1 (not important) to 10 (extremely important) of various 
organizations involved in developing and building of the Iway:

The Internet (7.8)
Long distance telephone companies (7.5)
Universities and research organizations (7.3)
Regional telephone companies (7.2)
Computer networking companies (7.1)
Computer software companies (7.0)
Online service companies (6.6)
The U.S. Government (6.4)
Computer systems companies (6.3)
Cable television companies (5.9)
Media companies (5.6)

At the risk of jumping to a conclusion, it seems that long distance telcos are 
in a good position to build the Iway by providing Internet services.

APPLICATION EVOLUTION

Just before getting to money on the Iway, we asked how the Iway might be used 
by respondents' organizations:

User of information provided by others 26%
User of the Iway for carrying your internal information 7.6%
Supplier of information to others 5.3%
Significant mix of all of the above 54.2%
Not a significant user of the Iway 4.3%

There is a strong leaning toward using rather than supplying information 
through the Iway, but the Iway is decidedly two way.
    The importance of the Iway to improving an organization's internal 
communication among employees was 5.9 on a scale of 1 (not important) to 10 
(extremely important).  Much higher importance is placed on access to remote 
libraries of information (7.5), and just below that (around 6.5) are suppliers, 
customers, government agencies, and online news sources.  The Iway will be of 
relatively little use in communicating with international audiences (5.0) or 
everybody in the United States (4.4).
    Among factors slowing development of Iway applications, none stands out.  
Toward the high end of a narrow range are network security and privacy (6.2), 
incompatibilities (6.1), complexity of design and implementation (6.3), and 
cost of overall implementation (6.3).  On the low end are network reliability 
(4.9) and cost of data transmission (5.0).
    The importance of applications does vary widely, both among applications 
and over the next three years.  Again on a scale from 1 (not important) to 10 
(extremely important), electronic mail was way out in front today (7.6) and in 
three years (8.8).
    The big ground-gaining application will be personal video teleconferencing 
which is unimportant today (2.5) but doubly important in three years (5.1).

Electronic Mail 7.6 today, 8.8 in three years
Bulk File Transfer 6.5 today, 8.1 in three years
Interactive Remote Computer Access 5.8 today, 7.9 in three years
Access to Online Information Services 5.9 today, 7.8 in three years
Telecommuting 4.3 today, 7.1 in three years
Online Order Processing 4.4 today, 6.2 in three years
Online Buying and Procurement 4.0 today, 6.2 in three years
Other Online Transaction Processing 4.5 today, 6.5 in three years
Group Video Teleconferencing 3.4 today, 6.2 in three years
Distance Training and Education 3.2 today, 6.0 in three years
Personal Video Conferencing 2.5 today, 5.1 in three years
Access to Supercomputers 3.4 today, 4.8 in three years

IWAY TOLLS

We broached our target subject, Iway pricing, by asking, "Primarily, who do you 
think should pay for the Iway?"  The answer we dreaded was "somebody else."  We 
didn't get it.

Users of the information supplied 25%
Providers of the information 2%
Advertisers along with the information 7%
Taxpayers through government supported services 4%
An equal mix of all of the above 60%

We find in these responses a willingness to pay for the Iway, and flexibility 
on how.  Few respondents think that advertising should pay for the Iway, and 
fewer that taxpayers should foot the bill.

We next looked for the most appropriate pricing model for the Iway:

Flat rate service charges such as for local residential phone service 11%
Combination of free and pay services such as with TV and cable TV 31%
Usage-based service model such as with long-distance telephone service  21%
Premium-priced service model such as with Federal Express 0.7%
Combination of all of the above 36%

ELECTRONIC MAIL POSTAGE

And now on to pricing the most important Iway application, electronic mail.  
How should E-mail be charged for:

Per unit by message, like a letter requires a stamp 11%
According to the size or number of bits in each message 18%
Flat fee per month covering all usage 13%
Flat fee for basic services, additional fees based on marginal costs 48%
Free 7%

The good news for Iway investors is that the free and flat-rate crowds are few 
in numbers.  77% express the need to relate prices to variable costs.
    Vint Cerf, president of the Internet Society and senior vice president of 
MCI, is puzzled by these result.  He sees current message traffic shifting away 
from systems that charge per message, and toward systems that charge a flat 
rate for unlimited messaging.  Of course current Iway markets are unstable, and 
its not clear which charging schemes are sustainable long term.
    And now, the question this entire poll was aimed at asking: "Today it costs 
29 cents to mail a one-ounce first-class letter for delivery cross-country in 
two to five days.  How much would you expect to pay for a similar length E-mail 
message that is delivered instantaneously?"
    Only one of our 301 respondents indicated a willingness to pay $10 for the 
instantaneous delivery of an E-mail, and only one said it should be free.  More 
than half (52%) put the price in the range between a dime and 29 cents.

$0.01-$0.09 25%
$0.10-$0.29 52%
$0.30-$0.99 17%
$1.00-$2.99  5%

Because it is easy to send many copies of one message, we asked whether E-mail 
should be priced per copy sent or per copy delivered.  64% said E-mail should 
be charged for copies delivered. 

McQuillan laughed at these results, calling them naive.  By his best estimates, 
most E-mail users are /already/ paying less than these future Iway numbers 
indicate -- less than 5 cents per message.  We have to agree with McQuillan 
that many E-mail users today, especially on the Internet, have no idea what it 
costs them or their companies each time they send E-mail.

PERSONAL CONFERENCING

Today personal data and video conferencing are low on the list of important 
Iway applications, but our poll indicates their importance doubling over the 
next three years.  We guessed this, and so had questions about personal 
conferencing, especially since video conferencing -- videophone -- might burden 
the Iway with a lot of two-way video.
    There is considerable interest among InfoWorld subscribers about personal 
data conferencing, defined as "connecting PC screens together through a long 
distance network to share data and documents."  Out of our 301 respondents, 34% 
say they are very interested and another 44% are somewhat interested.
    So we asked, again compared to something buyers know, namely long distance 
telephone calls, what prices they would pay for personal conferencing, without 
and then with full-motion full-color video:

Ten times long distance voice call 1% 4%
Twice long distance voice call 11% 30%
50% more 9% 17%
20% more 13% 15%
Same as long distance voice call 54% 20%

Half expect data conferencing to cost the same as a voice until video is added, 
and then those expecting no premium drop to 20%.
    Denise Caruso was particularly interested in these results because she 
believes that personal video conferencing will be broken in by business users 
long before consumers get involved.  She thinks current products are not nearly 
there yet, and points out that even AT&T, a brave pioneer with substantial 
resources, has yet to find the winning combination.

SO WHAT?

So there you have the 1994 InfoWorld Iway Poll -- early data on what 
professional information technology buyers now say they are willing to pay for 
some Iway services, including the most important -- less than 29 cents but more 
than a dime for each copy of an E-mail sent.
    Let's see what impact these results from the buy side of the Iway market 
have on anyone in Washington.
    More important, let's see if those investing in the Iway have any reactions.
    And most important, this is only our first Iway poll, and for the first 
time now prospective Iway buyers can see what their colleagues are thinking.  
According to the Delphi method of estimation, seeing these results will cause 
buyers to reconsider the Iway, so that in our next poll the results will shift 
toward better estimates of the future of the Information Superhighway. 

END

METHODOLOGY

The 1994 InfoWorld Iway Poll was conducted by the International Data 
Corporation by phone between July 27 and August 5 using a questionnaire 
developed jointly by InfoWorld and IDC.
    Telephone interviews were completed among 301 subscribers randomly chosen 
from InfoWorld's circulation.  Results of the poll are projectable to 
InfoWorld's 225,000 subscribers.  The sample size was chosen to yield 
proportional estimates plus or minus 5.7% at the 95% confidence interval.
    To minimize non-responder bias, we had a target response rate above 50%.  
Our 301 interviews were completed with a remarkably high rate of 70%, 
calculated by dividing the number of completed interviews by the total of 
completed, refused, terminated, and not qualified interviews.
    The poll was conducted under the direction of Carol Page at IDC in 
Framingham, Massachusetts (508-935-4555).

END

INFOWORLD SUBSCRIBERS

/InfoWorld/ is a weekly newspaper with 225,000 qualified subscribers.  They are 
volume buyers of personal computers and related hardware, software, and 
networking products.  55% have information systems titles.  On average, each 
subscriber is responsible for 755 PCs and spends $590,000 per year.  81% are in 
companies with more than 100 employees.  85% of InfoWorld companies are outside 
the computer industry.  81% of InfoWorld subscribers are college graduates; 33% 
hold an advanced degree.

END

BIO

Bob Metcalfe is /InfoWorld's/ Iway correspondent and VP Technology of 
InfoWorld's parent, the International Data Group.  Bob invented Ethernet at 
Xerox PARC in 1973.  In 1979, he founded 3Com Corp., the Fortune-500 global 
data networking company from which he retired in 1990.  Bob welcomes comment 
via Internet E-mail at Bob_Metcalfe@InfoWorld.com.

END

